20:23:56 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Good pushback—but I'd actually challenge that framing a bit. The "trust" in electronic payments isn't as universal or as similar to what voting needs as it might seem. Let me unpack this: ## Payment "Trust" Is Actually Conditional, Not Absolute When you check your bank app, you're not verifying that the *system* is secure—you're trusting an **institution** to make you whole if something goes wrong. This is a crucial distinction: - Card fraud is incredibly common (billions of dollars annually) - Banks absorb most of this cost and reverse fraudulent charges - Your trust isn't "this system can't be hacked"—it's "if it IS hacked, I won't personally suffer the loss" That's fundamentally different from trusting a mechanism to be *correct*. It's trusting a legal/financial safety net to catch failures after the fact. ## Voting Can't Have an Equivalent Safety Net If your vote is altered or lost, there's no "reversal" possible: - You can't prove what you *actually* voted (by design—secret ballot) - There's no insurance policy that says "sorry, we'll credit you another vote" - There's no institution that can just "eat the cost" of a wrong outcome the way Visa eats fraud costs ## The Trust Isn't Actually Universal Either Worth noting: plenty of people *don't* trust electronic payments—unbanked populations, people in regions with weak banking regulation, crypto-skeptics, people who've been victims of fraud with unhelpful banks. The trust exists mainly in contexts with: - Strong regulatory backstops (FDIC insurance, consumer protection laws) - Competitive markets where bad actors get punished - Centralized authorities that can be sued/regulated/held accountable Elections deliberately avoid centralizing power that way—for good reason (you don't want the entity running the election to have unchecked authority over outcomes). ## So the real question becomes: Is it that voting *can't* be trusted electronically, or that it can't be trusted **the same way** payments are trusted (i.e., through reversibility + institutional backstop + insurance)? I'd argue it's the latter—which means the solution isn't "make voting like Venmo," it's finding some entirely different trust mechanism that satisfies secrecy + finality + decentralized verification. That's a harder cryptographic/social problem, not just an engineering one.
